<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Seddon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as King Dick</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard Seddon was an English-born politician and the longest-serving prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, in office from 1893 until his death in 1906. Leading the Liberal government, he oversaw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-insurance">old-age pensions</a>, compulsory labour arbitration and land reform. He was a strong supporter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and sent New Zealand troops to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-seddon.jpg" alt="Richard Seddon" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herman John Schmidt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_Seddon_portrait_1905.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Richard John Seddon was born on 22 June 1845 at Eccleston, near St Helens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, the third of eight children of a headmaster and a teacher. He did poorly at school, left at 12, and trained as an engineer at foundries in Sutton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>. In 1862 he worked his passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> on the SS Great Britain and tried the railway workshops at Williamstown and the goldfields at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bendigo">Bendigo</a>. In 1866 he moved to the West Coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s South Island, worked the goldfields, kept a store and became a publican, and in 1869 he married Louisa Jane Spotswood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He entered local politics in 1870, sat on the Westland Provincial Council as an advocate for the miners, and in 1877 became the first Mayor of Kumara. Elected to Parliament in 1879, he was mocked for his accent and lack of education but became an authority on mining and a skilled obstructor of legislation. When John Ballance's Liberals took office in January 1891 he became minister of public works, mines, defence and marine, and after Ballance's death in April 1893 he was asked to form a ministry, avoiding a leadership vote against Robert Stout by arguing it would split the party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Women's suffrage passed in 1893 against his opposition, after his attempt to have the Legislative Council reject it failed. His Old-age Pensions Act of 1898 laid a basis for the later welfare state. His government bought large areas of land from Māori, and it raised the poll tax on Chinese immigrants to £100 a head in 1896; Seddon spoke of the Chinese in racist terms. After the Colonial Conference of 1897 he became known as a pillar of British imperialism; his hopes for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> and Samoa were checked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, but the Cook Islands came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> in 1901.</p><p>He took many portfolios himself and was accused of cronyism and of an autocratic style, which earned him the name King Dick. He led the Liberals to five consecutive election victories. He died of a heart attack at sea, returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> on the Oswestry Grange, on 10 June 1906, and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richard Seddon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1897 Premier <b>Richard Seddon</b> presented a copy of the words and music to Queen Victoria.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-defend-new-zealand">God Defend New Zealand</a></li><li><blockquote>The Liberal Party won; <b>Richard Seddon</b> became prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Richard Seddon's</b> Liberal government, in office from 1893, pioneered old-age pensions, compulsory labour arbitration and the purchase of large estates for small farmers.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-new-zealand">Colony of New Zealand</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Richard Seddon's</b> New Zealand government sought to expand in the Pacific.</blockquote><a href="/state/cook-islands-federation">Cook Islands Federation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eccleston, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Tasman Sea. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.45,-2.78&amp;year=1884" aria-label="Eccleston on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Richard Seddon"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4859 5556h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4859 5556h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.45,-2.78&amp;year=1884">What was here in 1884? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
